<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Schenectady, New York</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Schenectady is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> State on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-river">Mohawk River</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlers in 1661. On the night of 8–9 February 1690, during King William's War, a French and Native raiding party from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> surprised and burned the village, killing about 60 people. It later grew into an industrial city, the home of the General Electric company.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schenectady-new-york.jpg" alt="Schenectady, New York" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UpstateNYer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nott_SE_Landscape.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Schenectady is the county seat of Schenectady County, in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> near the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> and Hudson rivers. Albany, the state capital, lies about 24 km to the southeast, and with Albany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> the city is one of the three principal population and industrial centres of the Capital District. The name comes from the Mohawk word skahnéhtati, meaning beyond the pines, which was used for the area around Albany.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When Europeans first arrived, the Mohawk Valley was the territory of the Mohawk nation, one of the Five Nations of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy, who had held land in the region since at least 1100 CE. In the early 1600s the Mohawks moved their settlements closer to the river, and by 1629 they had taken over territory on the west bank of the Hudson that had belonged to the Algonquian-speaking Mahican. Dutch colonists, many of them from the Albany area, founded Schenectady on the south side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-river">Mohawk River</a> in the 17th century and laid out farms on strip plots along the river.</p><p>After the Revolutionary War the villagers reduced the power of the descendants of the early trustees and gained representative government. Members of the three oldest churches, the Dutch First Reformed Church, St. George's Episcopal Church and First Presbyterian Church, formed a union and founded Union College in 1795; it was the first private nondenominational institution of higher education in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-river">Mohawk River</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a> then tied Schenectady to the trade, manufacturing and transport corridor of the Mohawk Valley, and by 1824 more people worked in manufacturing than in agriculture or trade.</p><p>General Electric and the American Locomotive Company made the city a manufacturing centre known as the City that Lights and Hauls the World. In the early 20th century it drew immigrants from Eastern and Southern Europe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> leaving the rural South. General Electric's radio station WGY was the second commercial radio station in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and in 1928 its experimental station W2XB began the first regular television broadcasts in the country. The locomotive company's Schenectady plant closed in 1969.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Stockade Historic District, named after the stockade that once surrounded the colonial settlement, has dozens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English colonial houses of the 18th and 19th centuries. It was designated in 1965 as the first historic district in New York State. St. George's Episcopal Church dates from 1735, and the First Reformed Church was formed in the 17th century. Proctors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a>, built in 1926 as a vaudeville and movie theatre, was the site of one of the first public demonstrations of television.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Schenectady, New York” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governor Frontenac repulsed Sir William Phips’s attack on Quebec in 1690, and French and Native raiding parties struck English frontier settlements such as <b>Schenectady</b> in 1690 and Deerfield in 1704.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Military</a></li></ul></section>
